@docs/spec/sigil-config.sigil from SpecWorkspaceConfig import {
  language version,
  workspace config,
  workspace discovery,
  workspace root,
}
@docs/spec/sigil-glossary.sigil from SpecWorkspaceGlossary import {
  glossary occurrence,
  workspace glossary,
}
@docs/spec/sigil-reference.sigil from SpecLanguageReference import {
  diagnostic code,
  source document,
  source range,
}
@packages/core/core.sigil from SigilCore import {
  convention warning,
  formatted source,
  workspace conventions,
  workspace snapshot,
}

component SigilCli {
  goal {
    Give people and skills one command line, sigil, to check, format and
    inspect a Sigil workspace, and to start the language server.
  }

  constraints {
    SigilCli depends on SigilCore for the workspace snapshot and the formatted
    source, and must not read a source document by any other path.

    Except for the init command, SigilCli must find the workspace config by
    workspace discovery from the target path, or the current directory when
    there is none, or from an explicit workspace root.

    SigilCli must report every diagnostic with its diagnostic code and exit
    with exit status 1 when any diagnostic is an error.

    The fmt command must write nothing when any selected source document has an
    error that SigilCore refuses to format, and with its check option must
    change no file and must exit with exit status 1 when any file is
    noncanonical.

    The check command must report only the diagnostics of the workspace
    snapshot, and none about implementation sources.

    The check command must report each convention warning without changing the
    exit status, and must not read the write or judge parts of the
    workspace conventions.

    SigilCli must treat a removed command, graph, context, retrieve or render,
    as invalid usage and must not read the workspace for it.
  }

  interface {
    SigilCli provides the *init command*, which writes a new workspace config,
    keeps an existing workspace glossary, and refuses to overwrite a workspace
    config.

    SigilCli provides the *version command*, which reports the CLI version, the
    core version and the language version of the workspace.

    SigilCli provides the *parse command*, which reports one source document
    with a source range for each of its parts.

    SigilCli provides the *check command*, which reports the diagnostics of the
    workspace and their count for each severity.

    SigilCli provides the *fmt command*, which writes the formatted source of
    each selected source document that changed, validates every selected
    source document before it writes, and reports each file as formatted,
    unchanged, noncanonical or failed.

    SigilCli provides the *glossary command*, which reports the workspace
    glossary with each glossary occurrence it finds in the workspace.

    SigilCli provides the *lsp command*, which starts the language server on
    standard input and output for an editor. sigilc stays a separate
    executable and is not a sigil command.

    SigilCli provides the lsp command with exit status 0 when the editor asks it
    to shut down and then tells it to exit, or when its input ends, exit status
    1 when the editor tells it to exit without asking it to shut down, and exit
    status 3 when standard input or output fails.

    SigilCli provides the *usage text*, shown with exit status 0 for the help
    option alone or after any command, and the bare CLI version, followed by a
    newline, for the version option.

    SigilCli provides each command's *output format*, json, text, or markdown,
    except that the fmt command offers only json and text.

    SigilCli provides *exit status* 0 for success, 1 for an error diagnostic, 2
    for invalid usage and 3 for a runtime failure.

    SigilCli provides *invalid usage* as the refusal of an unknown command or
    option, with exit status 2 and nothing on standard output.
  }

  decisions {
    SigilCli keeps no language logic, so that every command and the language
    server read a Sigil workspace the way sigilc does.
  }
}
